:: steal your shadow. {"position":"374,1599","size":"100,100"}
//Have somebody steal your shadow.//
(set: $sequence to 8)
This is a thing that happened in Romania when people need to build a building.

It used to be that you could just wall somebody up alive in a building wall, and that would make the building really nice and strong.

Eventually, people realized this sort of architectural planning could get you arrested, so they started hiring shadow thieves to measure people's shadows with red string, after which they'd throw the red string in the wall foundation and the dude with his shadow stolen would die of "natural causes."

And then, presumably, become a vampire.


[[More Facts->Fascinating Vampire Facts]]


:: straw result {"position":"1154,1483","size":"100,100"}
(if: $strawShapes is "circles" or $strawShapes is "squares")[(go-to: "booth")](else:)[(if: $vampAtKitchen is false)[The vampire has started glancing at me.

He seems a little uncomfortable.](else:)[So many crosses.

I wish the vampire were here to see him, instead of off in the kitchen, making out with the cashier.]


[[Continue->crosses made of drinking straws]] ]


:: straw shapes {"position":"1154,1333","size":"100,100"}
(if: $strawShapes is "circles")[I take the straws and spend five minutes curling them into circles.

I make little slits in the ends so I can fold them into each other.

They're awkward, lumpy circles with corners. 

It's okay. I like awkward lumpy things. I spent my teenage years as an awkward lumpy thing.](if: $strawShapes is "squares")[I take the straws and spend five minutes folding them into squares.

I make little slits in the ends so I can fold them into each other.

I make four little folds in each straw to be the corners. 

The easiest shape is really a rectangle--that way I can take advantage of the pre-made corner at the top of the straw.

I am now surrounded by tiny squares.](if: $strawShapes is "crosses")[I take the straws and spend five minutes folding them into crosses.

I try to weave them together into a palm frond cross. I did this once in Sunday school. It is WAY harder than I remember.

Eventually I wuss out and make cheap, lazy crosses by twisting them apart at the bendy bit, cutting a slit near the top of the long piece, and shoving the small piece through the slit.

I end up with a lot of crosses.]


[[Continue->straw result]]


